<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:content="http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/"><channel><title>Phenomenology on metafunctor</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/tags/phenomenology/</link><description>Research Engineer &amp;amp; Computer Scientist - Machine Learning, Statistical Computing, Open Source Development</description><language>en-us</language><managingEditor>lex@metafunctor.com (Alex Towell)</managingEditor><webMaster>lex@metafunctor.com (Alex Towell)</webMaster><lastBuildDate>Thu, 09 Jul 2026 08:44:23 -0500</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://queelius.github.io/tags/phenomenology/" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><image><url>https://queelius.github.io/images/og-image.jpg</url><title>metafunctor</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/</link></image><item><title>Phenomenological Ethics: Starting From What Hurts</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-phenomenological-ethics/</link><pubDate>Tue, 04 Nov 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-phenomenological-ethics/</guid><description>When you stub your toe, you don&amp;#39;t consult moral philosophy to determine whether the pain is bad. The badness is immediate. Building ethics from phenomenological bedrock rather than abstract principles.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-phenomenological-ethics/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Phenomenology</media:title></media:content><category>philosophy</category><category>phenomenology</category><category>ethics</category><category>metaethics</category><category>experience</category><category>suffering</category><category>normative valence</category><category>AI alignment</category></item><item><title>The Map and the Territory: Why Metrics Miss Meaning</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-map-and-territory/</link><pubDate>Tue, 04 Nov 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-map-and-territory/</guid><description>Which is more fundamental, the heat you feel or the molecular motion you infer? Korzybski&amp;#39;s principle applied to AI alignment: why optimizing measurable proxies destroys the phenomenological reality those metrics were supposed to capture.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-map-and-territory/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Phenomenology</media:title></media:content><category>philosophy</category><category>metaphysics</category><category>phenomenology</category><category>epistemology</category><category>science</category><category>materialism</category><category>consciousness</category><category>AI alignment</category><category>measurement</category></item><item><title>Quality-Space and Consciousness-Primary Magic in Call of Asheron</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-12-call-of-asheron-quality-space/</link><pubDate>Mon, 20 Apr 2020 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-12-call-of-asheron-quality-space/</guid><description>Exploring how The Call of Asheron presents a radical alternative to mechanistic magic systems through quality-negotiation, direct consciousness-reality interaction, and bandwidth constraints as fundamental constants.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-12-call-of-asheron-quality-space/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Phenomenology</media:title></media:content><category>fiction</category><category>fantasy</category><category>philosophy</category><category>consciousness</category><category>phenomenology</category><category>qualia</category><category>magic</category></item><item><title>The Four Consciousness-Architectures: Why One Perspective Is Blindness</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-12-call-of-asheron-four-architectures/</link><pubDate>Wed, 15 Apr 2020 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-12-call-of-asheron-four-architectures/</guid><description>How The Call of Asheron uses four archetypal consciousness-types to explore the limits of any single perspective and the necessity of cognitive diversity for perceiving reality.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-12-call-of-asheron-four-architectures/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Phenomenology</media:title></media:content><category>fiction</category><category>fantasy</category><category>philosophy</category><category>consciousness</category><category>epistemology</category><category>cognitive-diversity</category><category>phenomenology</category></item><item><title>Bandwidth as Fundamental Constant: The 7±2 Limit in Call of Asheron</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-12-call-of-asheron-bandwidth/</link><pubDate>Fri, 10 Apr 2020 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-12-call-of-asheron-bandwidth/</guid><description>How The Call of Asheron treats working memory limitations not as neural implementation details but as fundamental constants governing consciousness-reality interaction through quality-space.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-12-call-of-asheron-bandwidth/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Phenomenology</media:title></media:content><category>fiction</category><category>fantasy</category><category>philosophy</category><category>consciousness</category><category>cognitive-science</category><category>working-memory</category><category>phenomenology</category></item></channel></rss>